WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:10.650
The cell cycle is a control system that
creates two accurate copies, or

2
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:15.730
daughter cells, from each mother cell.
For cells to replicate and stay more or

3
00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:20.930
less the same size, they have to double
in mass before they divide. To keep the

4
00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:26.850
same genetic material, the DNA must be
copied too. The cell cycle is divided

5
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:35.950
into four phases called G1, S, G2, and
M. During G1, or Gap 1 phase, cells

6
00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:40.780
accumulate biomass until they are
almost double their original size.

7
00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:47.330
Cells then commit to START. START
involves the activation of a central

8
00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:52.600
regulatory protein called cyclin
dependent kinase, or CDK, in an

9
00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:58.800
irreversible positive feedback loop.
Now cells enter S, or synthesis phase.

10
00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:04.129
CDK turns on thousands of protein
machines that make new DNA and rapidly

11
00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:10.720
churn out a copy of the genome. Once S
phase is done, cells may pause again in

12
00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:16.510
G2, or Gap 2 phase, to create more
biomass or correct any errors that

13
00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:22.010
happened when copying the DNA. Once
everything is ready, M phase, or

14
00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:27.060
mitosis, ensues. The chromosomes
condense and are captured by the

15
00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:32.000
mitotic spindle, which is a machine
that pulls them to opposite ends of the

16
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.230
cell. Finally, the cell divides between
the two duplicated genomes, creating

17
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.750
two daughters that are very similar to
their mother, but not always exactly,

18
00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:46.720
each with an identical copy of the
genome and half of the cell's contents.

19
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.080
A final note-- there are surveillance
systems, or checkpoints, in place in

20
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.290
case something goes wrong. If DNA
copying doesn't complete on time or if

21
00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:02.330
DNA damage occurs, then the DNA damage
checkpoint can delay the onset of

22
00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:06.300
metaphase. Furthermore, if the
chromosomes are not correctly attached

23
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.190
to the spindle, the process of
chromosome division is also delayed.

24
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:15.450
Completing the cell cycle correctly is
crucial for our bodies to live and grow.

